intro.bodyMarkdown:
Sometimes work must be finished off whether or not things went wrong: hide a "Loading…" message, close a file, unlock a button. A **`finally`** block runs after `try` (and `catch`, if there was an error) **every time**.

```
function load(text) {
  console.log("Loading…")
  try {
    return JSON.parse(text)
  } catch {
    return null
  } finally {
    console.log("Done loading")
  }
}

console.log(load("[1]"))
console.log(load("oops"))
```

Notice that `finally` runs even when the `try` or `catch` **returns**: the return value waits until `finally` has finished, then goes back to the caller.

You can also use `try`…`finally` with no `catch`. The error isn't handled, so it still goes up to the caller, but the clean-up happens first:

```
let busy = false
function save(data) {
  busy = true
  try {
    if (!data) throw new Error("Nothing to save")
    return "saved"
  } finally {
    busy = false
  }
}

try {
  save(null)
} catch (e) {
  console.log(e.message, "| busy:", busy)
}
```

Keep `finally` for clean-up only. Don't `return` from it: a `return` in `finally` replaces whatever the `try` returned or threw, which hides errors.

